            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Dian Fossey</strong><br>\nDian Fossey was born on January 16, 1932, in San Francisco, California. From a young age, she was fascinated by animals and dreamed of becoming a veterinarian. However, her grades were not good enough for veterinary school, so she studied occupational therapy at San Jose State College. After graduating, Fossey worked at a hospital in California and later in Kentucky, where she treated children with polio.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Journey to Africa</strong><br>\nFossey's life changed direction after reading about the work of famous anthropologist Louis Leakey. Inspired by his research, she took out a bank loan and went on a six-week trip to Africa in 1963. It was during this trip that she first observed mountain gorillas in the wild at a distance. This experience ignited a passion in Fossey to learn more about gorillas and their conservation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Meeting Louis Leakey</strong><br>\nUpon her return, Fossey met Louis Leakey at a lecture in Louisville, Kentucky. Leakey believed studying primates could provide insights into the evolution of humans and was looking for candidates to research great apes. Impressed with her enthusiasm and initiative, he encouraged Fossey to pursue long-term field research on mountain gorillas.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
